 **Strengths:** Provides quick insight into isosurface quality without additional sampling or solving linear systems [S1]. The vertex approximation $e$ is significantly closer to the measured error than the standard bound $e_b$ [S1].
 **Limitations:** It only estimates error for existing crossings; it does not detect missing features (hidden features) on its own [S1]. The standard bound $e_b$ is a loose overestimate [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The method originates from the analysis of interpolation errors in volume rendering and isosurface extraction. It builds on the observation that Taylor series expansion can estimate local function error [S1]. Previous work by Moller et al. used Taylor series for smooth filters and error estimation in volume data approximation [S1]. This specific application to edge-crossing error in Marching Cubes algorithms was proposed to visualize isosurface discrepancies and guide decisions on using higher-order interpolation or higher-resolution data [S1].
